From b8615dbb83e74d7e9f00de64470474f64eeed627 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 06:59:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] pdlc: run each stage of ship in a new pdlc:stage agent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Before this change, ship ran every stage in one conversation, and the context grew from
stage to stage. Now ship starts a new pdlc:stage agent for each stage. The prompt of the
agent has four lines: the project root, the stage, the path of the skill file, and the
work. The agent reads the skill file and does the stage with the recommended answers.

The stage skills stay unforked. Each skill still works alone, with and without defaults.

Each fact that a stage took from the conversation now comes from a file:
- Build writes the reason for a wrong check in the handoff of the change. The title is
  build to tests, or build to acceptance.
- Acceptance finds a send-back in the handoff of the change at the top of the stack.
- The rounds come from the commits of the branch. A stop commit starts a new count.
- A stage takes the recommended answer of an earlier stop from the latest handoff.
- Ship reads the link and the GIF of each change from the handoff `verify → user`.

The agent stops for two cases that take no default: a contradiction between requirements,
and a file outside the Files of a change. Ship keeps its stop list, its rounds, the order
of its changes, and its merge proposal.

The guard needs no code change, because the role stage matches no lane. A test shows this.
The e2e bench and the vet eval did not run for this change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
